    "abstract": "<jats:p>The Kalman smoother is known to be the maximum likelihood estimator when the measurement and transition functions are affine; i.e., a linear function plus a constant. A new proof of this result is presented that shows that the Kalman smoother decomposes a large least squares problem into a sequence of much smaller problems. The iterated Kalman smoother is then presented and shown to be a Gauss–Newton method for maximizing the likelihood function in the nonaffine case. This method takes advantage of the decomposition obtained with the Kalman smoother.</jats:p>",
